# Administrative History: Gettysburg National Military Park and National Cemetery, Pennsylvania (July 1991): second reading of PDF pp. 19-20, workstream bt-web, 2026-11-15.
# The first reading took 21 passages (monuments, the Warren survey, the commemorative periods). PDF pp. 19-20 quote two contemporary accounts: M. Jacobs, "Later Rambles Over the Field of Gettysburg", United States Service Magazine, January 1864 (the study's note 3), and the letter of David Wills to Governor Curtin of July 24, 1863 (printed in Bartlett, The Soldiers' National Cemetery at Gettysburg, 1874; the study's note 4). Each extract says whose words it quotes.
# The PDF of this report (210 MB) is not kept locally and the digits of these two pages were not read from a page image (the text layer is a clean OCR of a printed book); the extracts say so by carrying page_image_viewed false.
@ NPS-ADHI
%basis Jacobs, "Later Rambles Over the Field of Gettysburg" (January 1864); Wills to Curtin, July 24, 1863 (printed in Bartlett, 1874), as the study names them

19 | damage | The battlefield a week or more after the battle: trees scarred and shattered, shot and shell lying about, fences swept away, houses shattered, fields trodden down like a highway (Jacobs, January 1864) | - | 1863-07 (a week or more after the battle; account published January 1864) | stated | The study quotes a visitor's account (Jacobs, January 1864) that after the wounded and dead were removed trees were scarred and shattered, thousands of minie balls and shells lay scattered over the ground and fences were swept away, houses shattered and the fields trodden down like a highway | #s13-15 | #s19
19 | damage | About three thousand dead horses lay singly and in groups over the field, by the testimony of an army officer (Jacobs, January 1864) | - | 1863-07 (after the battle) | stated | The study quotes Jacobs, who gives the testimony of an army officer that the carcasses of about three thousand horses, most killed by shells and shot from the artillery, lay singly and in groups and filled the air for miles around | #s12
20 | earthworks | Earthworks thrown up for the artillery of about forty pieces on the Baltimore turnpike opposite the cemetery (Wills, July 24, 1863) | - | 1863-07-02 to 1863-07-03 (as David Wills described the place in his letter of July 24, 1863) | stated | The study quotes David Wills's letter of July 24, 1863 that the elevated ground on the Baltimore turnpike opposite the cemetery was where the army had about forty pieces of artillery in action all Thursday and Friday and for their protection had thrown up a large number of earthworks for the artillerists | #s4 | #s22
20 | cemetery | The two lots proposed for the burial ground in July 1863: about three and a half acres of Raffensperger and four and a half of Menchy, eight acres, at $200 an acre (Wills, July 24, 1863) | - | 1863-07 (letter of July 24, 1863) | stated | The study quotes David Wills's letter of July 24, 1863 that the elevated ground made two lots of eight acres together, about three and a half acres belonging to Mr. Raffensperger and four and a half to Mr. Menchy, which the owners would sell for the burial ground at $200 an acre each | #s8-9 | #s22
